{"generation_id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f","teaching_plan":{"arc":"The lesson opens on the classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and asks learners to commit to a guess about where its food comes from, so a personal wrong model is on the table. The explanation then fixes the category term — the plant is a producer, a living thing that makes its own food — and separates what the plant takes in through its roots from what it makes. The contrast section puts the producer idea against the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings on the same plant so the boundary is visible, and the close checks whether the learner now recognizes the plant, not the soil or the water, as the maker of the food.","revision":1,"sections":[{"blocks":[{"id":"orient-b1","brief":"Present the concrete case: a potted classroom plant on the windowsill, sitting next to a cup of water. Ask learners where they think the plant's food comes from and have them commit to one answer before anything is explained. Do not name soil, water, or sunlight as food, and do not state the target idea that the plant makes its own food.","intent":"orient","evidence":"The objective's recognition claim only becomes meaningful if the learner first holds the concrete windowsill plant and a personal guess to revise, and this is the only place the anchor can be introduced.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["anchor.anchor-1","lesson.objective","slot.orient.purpose","prior-2"],"learner_action":{"action":"select-one","target":"where the windowsill plant's food comes from","purpose":"elicit the learner's initial belief so the explanation and contrast sections have a specific wrong model to work against","difficulty":"guided","expected_evidence":"a committed first answer, which reveals whether the learner already places food-making inside the plant or attributes it to soil or water"},"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[],"stimulus_dependencies":[]}],"slot_id":"orient","transition":null,"specific_purpose":"Put the windowsill plant in front of the learner and get an initial commitment about where its food comes from, so the explanation has something to work against."},{"blocks":[{"id":"explain-b1","brief":"Define producer for a plant: a producer is a living thing that makes its own food. State plainly that the windowsill plant is a producer, so its food is made by the plant itself and is not something delivered to it from outside. Keep the definition to this single idea and do not yet describe how the plant makes the food or what role sunlight plays.","intent":"define","evidence":"The objective's verb is 'recognize' a category, not explain a mechanism, so the slot needs the category term 'producer' fixed precisely before any contrast with soil or water can mean anything.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","slot.explain.purpose","anchor.anchor-1","prior-2"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[],"stimulus_dependencies":[]},{"id":"explain-b2","brief":"Build the idea on the same windowsill plant: the roots draw soil materials and water into the plant, but what enters through the roots is raw material the plant uses, not the plant's food. The food itself is made by the plant. Hold the learner's attention on the plant as the maker and treat soil and water only as intakes, so the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings are undermined without being named as errors yet. Do not introduce the sunlight-as-food idea and do not treat food and energy as interchangeable.","intent":"explain","evidence":"Two approved misconceptions both locate food outside the plant, so this block must relocate food-making inside it by separating 'what the plant takes in' from 'what the plant makes' — a separation a bare definition cannot make.","position":1,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","slot.explain.purpose","anchor.anchor-1","prior-1","M1","M2"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":[],"stimulus_dependencies":[]}],"slot_id":"explain","transition":"Learners have committed to an answer about where the windowsill plant's food comes from, so they are ready to hear what actually happens rather than guess again.","specific_purpose":"Establish that the plant is a producer: it makes its own food, while soil materials and water are things it takes in rather than food."},{"blocks":[{"id":"contrast-b1","brief":"Take the windowsill plant and put the soil-as-food reading beside the target: one learner says the plant's food is the soil, pulled up through the roots; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and only draws materials from the soil. Show why the soil reading cannot stand, since the plant still makes food inside itself and the soil is only one of its intakes. Do not repeat the earlier intake-versus-made distinction in full, and do not slide into the water-as-food reading here.","intent":"diagnose-misconception","evidence":"The slot's typical intents all treat both sides as legitimate or as a slip; M1 is a held belief that must be surfaced and shut down, so an atypical intent is required and the slot purpose still matches exactly.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["M1","anchor.anchor-1","slot.contrast.purpose","lesson.objective"],"learner_action":{"action":"select-one","target":"which reading of the windowsill plant matches the producer idea: food made by the plant, or food taken from the soil","purpose":"surface whether the learner still holds the soil-as-food model after hearing the target idea","difficulty":"guided","expected_evidence":"selection of the producer reading rather than the soil reading, revealing whether M1 has been displaced"},"departure_reason":"M1 is a held belief, not a legitimate alternative and not a slip, so compare and warn cannot test it. Diagnose-misconception is the only shape that surfaces a named wrong model and shuts it down, and no typical intent for this slot does that.","source_question_ids":["7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i2"],"stimulus_dependencies":[]},{"id":"contrast-b2","brief":"Set the water-as-food reading against the target on the same windowsill plant beside the cup of water: one learner says the plant's food is the water we give it, since water is what we pour in; the producer idea says the plant makes its own food and the water is an intake, not the food. Make clear that pouring more water does not feed the plant in the sense of food, and that the plant remains the maker. Do not re-run the soil argument and do not treat sunlight as food or as energy here.","intent":"diagnose-misconception","evidence":"M2 is a second named belief that places food outside the plant, and because the anchor already includes the cup of water, this block can test that belief against a case the learner has held since the opening instead of a fresh example.","position":1,"evidence_refs":["M2","anchor.anchor-1","slot.contrast.purpose","lesson.objective"],"learner_action":null,"departure_reason":"M2 is a held belief with a confident wrong answer available, so warn (a slip) and compare (two legitimate sides) both misfit. Diagnose-misconception is the only shape that tests this named wrong model against the target.","source_question_ids":["7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i3"],"stimulus_dependencies":[]}],"slot_id":"contrast","transition":"Learners now hold the claim that the plant makes its own food and merely takes in soil materials and water, so they are ready to see that claim tested against the readings that would put the food in the soil or in the water.","specific_purpose":"Set the producer idea against the two near-miss readings of the same plant so learners see where the boundary of the idea lies."},{"blocks":[{"id":"check-b1","brief":"Pose one check that asks the learner to apply the producer idea to the windowsill plant: given that the plant has soil around its roots and water poured in, decide where the plant's food comes from and confirm that the plant is the thing that makes it. Keep the check to this single recognition of the plant as producer, and do not re-explain soil or water as intakes.","intent":"check-understanding","evidence":"The objective is a recognition claim about which category the plant belongs to, so the close must read whether the learner places food-making in the plant rather than in the soil or water — exactly the correct-versus-incorrect signal check-understanding is for.","position":0,"evidence_refs":["lesson.objective","slot.check.purpose","anchor.anchor-1"],"learner_action":{"action":"select-one","target":"where the windowsill plant's food comes from and whether the plant is a producer","purpose":"measure whether the producer idea displaced the soil-as-food and water-as-food readings after the lesson","difficulty":"independent","expected_evidence":"selection of the response that identifies the plant as the maker of its own food, showing the objective has landed"},"departure_reason":null,"source_question_ids":["7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i1"],"stimulus_dependencies":[]}],"slot_id":"check","transition":"Learners have seen the producer idea held against both the soil reading and the water reading on the same plant, so the close can now measure which reading they carry out.","specific_purpose":"Check whether the learner now recognizes the plant as a producer that makes its own food, rather than something fed by soil or water."}],"anchor_usage":[{"usage":"Introduce the potted classroom plant on the windowsill beside the cup of water and ask where its food comes from, so learners hold one concrete case and one committed guess.","slot_id":"orient"},{"usage":"Build the producer idea on the same plant, naming what enters through its roots as raw material and identifying the plant itself as the thing 