a cup of water; learners track what the pla…"},"lesson":{"subject":"Science","objective":"Recognize that plants are producers that make their own food","grade_level":"Grade","lesson_mode":"first_exposure","knowledge_type":"conceptual","path_lesson_id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f"},"limits":{"max_sections":4,"max_total_blocks":10,"max_blocks_per_section":3},"resource_id":"lesson","approved_items":[{"id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i1","stem":"A bean plant is planted in rich, dark potting soil. Over several months it grows tall and produces new leaves. Which statement best explains where the substance of the new leaves came from?","card_id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Most of the new leaf material came from materials in the soil absorbed by the roots.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"Most of the new leaf material came from gases in the air that the plant used to make its own food.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"Most of the new leaf material came from water that was poured into the soil.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"Most of the new leaf material came from sunlight striking the leaves each day.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M3","teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":"M1","b":null,"c":"M2","d":"M3"},"correct_key":"b"},{"id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i2","stem":"A student sets up three identical bean plants. Plant P gets air but no soil, and is watered with a nutrient solution. Plant Q gets soil but no air for its leaves, and is watered normally. Plant R gets air and soil, and is watered normally. All three get sunlight. Which plant is most able to make its own food, and why?","card_id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0","options":[{"key":"a","text":"Plant Q, because it has soil to take in food through its roots.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"Plant P, because it receives the nutrient solution instead of plain water.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"Plant R, because it has all the conditions plants normally use to make their own food.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"Plant R, because it receives the most sunlight and sunlight is its food.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M3","teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":"M1","b":"M2","c":null,"d":"M3"},"correct_key":"c"},{"id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i3","stem":"A gardener notices that a plant kept in a dark cupboard stops growing and turns yellow, even though it is watered regularly and potted in fertile soil. Which explanation best fits what happens to a producer?","card_id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0","options":[{"key":"a","text":"The plant cannot get food because the soil is no longer supplying it in the dark.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"The plant cannot make its food because it needs light energy to do so.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"The plant cannot get food because the water it receives is not enough on its own.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"The plant cannot get food because the darkness removes the energy stored in its leaves.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M3","teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":"M1","b":null,"c":"M2","d":"M3"},"correct_key":"b"},{"id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i4","stem":"Which statement best explains why a plant is called a producer?","card_id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0","options":[{"key":"a","text":"It pulls ready-made food from the soil and stores it in its leaves.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"It takes in water and uses that water as its only food.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"It makes its own food using light energy, water, and air.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"It absorbs sunlight directly as food and stores it for later use.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M3","teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":"M1","b":"M2","c":null,"d":"M3"},"correct_key":"c"},{"id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0.i5","stem":"A student claims: 'Plants do not really make food; they just take in what they need from the soil and water.' Which evidence would best challenge this claim?","card_id":"7fc2a387-b963-42ed-8fa7-b7a40a71cf0f:fce6a245-18bb-4cdf-9fc9-0d81a6f2ebb0","options":[{"key":"a","text":"A plant grown in water without soil can still grow and make food.","correct":true,"diagnoses":null,"teacher_edited":false},{"key":"b","text":"A plant with more soil around its roots grows larger than one with less soil.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M1","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"c","text":"A plant given extra water grows taller than one given less water.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M2","teacher_edited":false},{"key":"d","text":"A plant placed in brighter light grows faster than one in dim light.","correct":false,"diagnoses":"M3","teacher_edited":false}],"diagnoses":{"a":null,"b":"M1","c":"M2","d":"M3"},"correct_key":"a"}],"misconceptions":[{"id":"M1","statement":"{'id': 'M1', 'description': \"Learners think a plant's food comes from the soil through its roots, so soil is the plant's food.\", 'source': 'drafted'}"},{"id":"M2","statement":"{'id': 'M2', 'description': \"Learners think plants feed only on the water we give them, treating water alone as the plant's food.\", 'source': 'drafted'}"},{"id":"M3","statement":"{'id': 'M3', 'description': \"Learners treat food and energy as the same thing, so sunlight counts as the plant's food because it makes the plant grow.\", 'source': 'drafted'}"}],"prior_established":[{"id":"prior-1","statement":"plants have roots, stems and leaves"},{"id":"prior-2","statement":"living things need food to grow"}]},"catalogue":{"version":"1.1.0","form_projection_hash":null,"teaching_projection_hash":"a9530874d702efc409dcd4c87658fe037981b8a8a6ed76e1d95a551ff8885870"}}